{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J7", "U9"],
      "pins": ["J7.A4", "J7.B4", "U9.1", "U9.2"],
      "nets": ["VBUS", "+5V"],
      "observed": "On the MCU sheet the USB-C receptacle J7's VBUS pin (A4) is wired to the red net label 'VBUS' (the power-mux output / pre-buck rail), not to '+5V'. On the Power sheet the TPS2121 mux U9 has OUT (pin 1) = VBUS and IN2 (pin 2) = +5V, so VBUS is the post-mux rail that can rise to ~12 V when the barrel jack is present.",
      "expected": "The spec states the mux IN2 must be fed from the USB 'VBUS pins (A4/A9/B4/B9) → +5V net', and that 'this net [VBUS] is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux'. The USB-C VBUS pin should carry +5V (mux input), never the mux output VBUS.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "IN2 (pin 2) wired to USB +5V/VBUS, with a common OUT feeding the board's unregulated VBUS rail ... note this net is named VBUS in the netlist but is distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "mcu.kicad_sch (tile 2/4): J7 USB_C_Receptacle, pin A4 connected by a green wire to red label 'VBUS'; power.kicad_sch: U9 TPS2121RUXR OUT pin1 = VBUS, IN2 pin2 = +5V."}
      ],
      "impact": "The USB-C VBUS pin is tied to the mux output instead of the mux input. With the 12 V barrel jack connected, the mux output (~12 V) appears on the USB-C VBUS pins, back-powering any attached host/device (potential damage), and the mux's seamless +5V/+12V arbitration is defeated because +5V is merged with VBUS; USB alone can no longer correctly power the board.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The board's power-mux architecture is miswired: the USB-C VBUS pin is connected to the post-mux VBUS rail rather than to the +5V mux input, which the spec explicitly keeps separate; this would put the 12 V mux output onto the USB port and break the input arbitration. Reference designators are renumbered versus the spec but that is not itself an anomaly."
}

